Chapter: RealToIntWide

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 real-to-int and real-approx-to-int on magnitudes ABOVE 2^31, which is the
 range a 32-bit conversion instruction cannot express and silently saturates
 in. It is here because the riscv encoder emitted exactly that instruction:
 rv-fcvt-l-d passed rs2 = 0, which under funct7 0x61 is FCVT.W.D, the 32-bit
 form, where FCVT.L.D is rs2 = 2. Every value below answered 2147483647 or
 -2147483648 on riscv while x86-64 and arm64 answered correctly.

 EVERY VALUE HERE IS UNAMBIGUOUS ON PURPOSE, and that is what makes this test
 safe to grade on all three backends. No NaN, no infinity, and nothing outside
 i64, because those three cases are where x86 (integer indefinite), arm64
 (saturate) and riscv (saturate) genuinely disagree by ISA and the language
 has not ruled which it means. A test carrying one of them would encode one
 machine's answer as the truth for all of them.

 The last line is the one that matters most: show on a Real routes through the
 same conversion in the runtime's double-to-string path, so a 32-bit
 instruction there corrupts printing rather than only arithmetic.

Section: Report

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("three-bil    " & show (real-to-int 3000000000.0))
    print-line-uni ("ten-bil      " & show (real-to-int 10000000000.0))
    print-line-uni ("one-tril     " & show (real-to-int 1000000000000.0))
    print-line-uni ("neg-three    " & show (real-to-int (0.0 - 3000000000.0)))
    print-line-uni ("at-2p31-less " & show (real-to-int 2147483647.0))
    print-line-uni ("at-2p31      " & show (real-to-int 2147483648.0))
    print-line-uni ("roundtrip    " & show (real-to-int (real-from-int 3000000000)))
    print-line-uni ("approx-wide  " & show (real-approx-to-int (to-real-approx 3000000000.0)))
    print-line-uni ("show-real    " & show 3000000000.5)
  end
